00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  there are signs this morning of a  potential breakthrough for many people  suffering from long-term  covet symptoms the facebook group  survivor corps found that 39 percent of  covet long-haulers  report their lingering symptoms improved  after they were vaccinated  cbs news senior medical correspondent dr  taran narula spoke with  two patients who got better dr narula  good morning to you  good morning tony many long-haulers say  they're terrified of getting the coveted  vaccine  worried that the shot meant to protect  them will make the debilitating symptoms  they now live with worse  but many patients are reporting the  opposite after their shots  i was extremely fatigued i was having  these excruciating pains in my back and  in my lungs almost kimberly  wills-rinaldi says  she hasn't been the same since she was  diagnosed with covid last march the  58-year-old long-hauler suffered from  serious lingering health issues until  she received her first coveted vaccine  shot  last month for me this is a miracle  what has been the improvement in your  symptoms  the viral conjunctivitis specifically in  my right eye  is gone the rash that was on my back  and on my arms and my neck that's gone  the extreme extreme  fatigue episodes those have gotten  better too  the group's survivor corps polled 962  long-haulers  and found 46 percent said they remained  the same after the vaccine  14 percent felt worse while 39 percent  improved after the shot from mild to  full resolution of symptoms  survivors reported feeling better at  various points of the vaccination  process  it wasn't something i had expected to  see the poll  caught the attention of akiko iwasaki an  immunobiologist at yale school of  medicine  she's launching a study on people with  long covid collecting  blood and saliva samples to compare  immune responses before  and after long-haulers get the vaccine  to understand  if it really helps one long covet theory  is that pieces of the virus may  hide in the body the vaccine induces  robust  antibody and t cell responses that can  clear the viral reservoir  or remnants that's causing the  inflammation  and that would be a permanent solution  to lung coveted another theory  long covet is driven by a hyperactive  autoimmune response  and the vaccine may reduce those  responses there is no good therapy for  lung covid  and the people have really debilitating  symptoms and so  there's a lot of excitement about this  study  i was terrified of getting the vaccine  at the same time i was terrified not to  get the vaccine  judy dodd suffered dizziness headaches  and exhaustion for more than a year  after she got covered a few weeks after  i got the second vaccine  i was like in my living room like  dancing or something and my partner was  like  i don't think i've seen you dance in a  year i forgot what it was like to like  wake up in the morning and feel good and  feel  you know excited for the day and not  feel defeated  before you've even gotten out of bed it  sounds like someone just  switched a light on that's how i felt  that's exactly how i felt like  yale hopes to enroll at least a hundred  people with long covid in its study to  understand these  phenomena and see if the relief will  last researchers also say the findings  could help in  other diseases that may be triggered by  a virus such as chronic fatigue syndrome  tony very interesting so what more are  researchers learning about the  connection between the vaccine and the  covert long-haul symptoms  well what a fascinating twist this would  be and you know there is precedent for  therapeutic vaccines we don't think  about vaccines in that way but there are  three approved right now  for cancer there are others being  studied for hiv so we  these are anecdotes in terms of  long-haulers and covid we really need  those observational controlled studies  but  researchers believe this really is a  heterogeneous disease meaning for some  people it may be those viral reservoirs  or remnants  for others it may be the autoimmune  phenomenon or for some all three so  they really need to categorize patients  to really better understand them to  figure out a targeted approach and that  means looking at their antibody response  t cell response cytokine response  with those blood and saliva samples and  and also understanding  does the vaccine maybe work better in  long haulers who are women versus men or  different age groups or different  ethnicities  but really the dream and the hope is  that this can provide that therapy for  the so many americans that are suffering  with long-haul syndrome yeah really  tantalizing stuff tara thank you very  much we appreciate

